zjot's own cases, in the format of the vendored djot.js files (see test_runner.zig).

Every expected output in this file was produced by djot.js 0.3.2 on 2026-10-23:

    npx -y @djot/djot@0.3.2 input.dj                 (HTML cases)
    npx -y @djot/djot@0.3.2 -t ast -p input.dj       (`ap` cases, JSON rendered to this
                                                      file's AST text form by
                                                      test/tools/djot2ast.py, checked
                                                      against every vendored `a`/`ap` case)

Expectations are never written by hand. A case goes in only when zjot and djot.js
agree on it; where zjot deliberately differs from djot.js, the difference is written
down here instead of pinned as a case.

Deliberate differences from djot.js 0.3.2:

1. Block attributes do not survive a blank line, and a block attribute left pending
   when a container (block quote, list item, fenced div, footnote) ends is dropped.
   djot.js 0.3.2 applies `{.c}`, blank, `para` to the paragraph and carries `> {.c}`
   out of the quote onto the next block, but its own test suite (attributes.test,
   "If there is a blank line between, the attributes won't be applied") says it must
   not; zjot follows the suite. Checked 2026-10-23.

2. A footnote reference's label is not part of a heading's text for its id:
   `# Introduction[^1]` gets `id="Introduction"`, as footnotes.test expects; djot.js
   0.3.2 itself produces `Introduction1`. zjot follows the suite. Checked 2026-10-24.

HTML, pending (not deliberate): zjot writes an element's `id` before its other attributes;
djot.js keeps the source order (`{k=v .a #i}` gives `k="v" class="a" id="i"`). Deferred.

AST text form (`--ast`), where zjot's output differs from djot.js's `-t ast` as the
converter renders it, so no `ap` case can pin it:

3. Offsets and columns count bytes; djot.js counts UTF-16 code units (`é _é_` gives
   `emph (1:3:2-…)` there and `(1:4:3-…)` here).
4. Generated ids are printed on headings and sections; djot.js prints explicit ones only.
5. Smart punctuation prints as `ellipsis`, `em_dash`, `en_dash` and curly-quote `str`
   nodes where djot.js prints `smart_punctuation`.
6. An attribute on a word wraps it in a `span`; djot.js puts the attribute on the `str`.
7. Reference links print the resolved `destination`; djot.js prints the `reference` only.
8. `raw_block` and `raw_inline` print the format as `lang`; djot.js's `format` is not
   rendered by the converter.
9. djot.js 0.3.2 drops the text before an inline image (`a ![alt](u)` loses `a `); zjot
   keeps it, so only an image at the start of a paragraph is pinned.
10. djot.js 0.3.2 adds an empty `str` with inverted positions after a cell whose content
    ends in a container inline (`| _a_ |` gives `str (1:6:5-1:5:4)`); zjot does not, so
    cells are pinned with plain text.

Ordered lists in the AST text form print neither `start` nor `type`, as djot.js's text
form (its JSON has `start` and `style`); the HTML renders both from the list's markers,
before any attributes. Attributes print as id, class, then the rest in source order, as
djot.js's text form (the HTML keeps source order, see the HTML difference below).

Footnotes in the AST text form: a reference prints its label, and every footnote
definition follows the document as a `footnote` block with its `label` and span, in
definition order. djot.js's own `-t ast` prints no footnotes; the converter renders its
JSON `footnotes` map the same way, in that map's key order, which in JavaScript puts
integer-like labels such as `1` first whatever the definition order (so `ap` cases use
labels that are not numbers).


Heading ids: generated ids follow document order wherever the heading sits.
````
# Foo

> # Foo
.
<section id="Foo">
<h1>Foo</h1>
<blockquote>
<h1 id="Foo-1">Foo</h1>
</blockquote>
</section>
````

Heading ids: an explicit id met later does not rename an id already generated.
````
# Foo bar

{#Foo-bar}
Paragraph
.
<section id="Foo-bar">
<h1>Foo bar</h1>
<p id="Foo-bar">Paragraph</p>
</section>
````

Footnote heading ids follow the endnotes' order, not the definitions'.
````
[^a]:
    > # P

[^b]:
    > # Q

text[^b] [^a]
.
<p>text<a id="fnref1" href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a> <a id="fnref2" href="#fn2" role="doc-noteref"><sup>2</sup></a></p>
<section role="doc-endnotes">
<hr>
<ol>
<li id="fn1">
<blockquote>
<h1 id="Q">Q</h1>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="#fnref1" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p>
</li>
<li id="fn2">
<blockquote>
<h1 id="P">P</h1>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="#fnref2" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
````

